WHAT WAS TAKEN. Eighteen glyphs, across two modules.

`src/data/transitVehicleIcons.js` draws live French transit vehicles by class
(retrieved 2026-08-31): airport_shuttle, cable_car, directions_boat,
directions_bus, directions_railway, flight, funicular, local_taxi, monorail,
subway, train, tram, trolley.

`src/data/sharedMobilityIcons.js` draws shared vehicles by form factor
(retrieved 2026-09-01): pedal_bike, electric_bike, electric_scooter,
electric_moped, directions_car.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. Nothing, in the artwork.

Only the single `d` path string of each glyph is vendored, verbatim and
unmodified, into those modules — the surrounding SVG document is not, and the
coordinates are NOT rescaled: both modules carry Material's own
`0 -960 960 960` viewBox precisely so that this paragraph stays true.

At render time each path is drawn TWICE by this project: once as a wide dark
stroke (a halo, so the glyph stays readable over pale terrain) and once as a
white fill (so Cesium's per-billboard colour multiply can tint it — by vehicle
class in the transit layer, by operator in the shared-mobility one). The path
geometry itself is untouched.

TWO SHAPES IN `sharedMobilityIcons.js` ARE NOT GOOGLE'S and carry no claim
under this notice: the `other` disc (drawn when a feed does not say what a
vehicle is) and the `station` dock rack, both authored by this project in the
same coordinate box. Material's own `bike_dock` was evaluated for the latter
and rejected on legibility — it renders as a bollard at the size this layer
draws.

